\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Counting attacks and the free-witness question}

A recurring template in attacks on integer factorization is the \emph{counting
attack}. Given a composite $N$, one constructs a combinatorial or arithmetic
quantity---the number of lattice points on a circle whose radius depends on $N$,
the number of roots of a polynomial modulo $N$, the number of representations by
a binary quadratic form of discriminant tied to $N$, the number of cusps of a
modular curve of level $N$, the minimum distance of a Reed--Solomon-type code
indexed by $N$---and hopes that the resulting integer carries recoverable
information about the prime factors.

Empirically these attacks fall into two piles. Either the aggregate depends only
on $N$ and not on how $N$ splits, in which case it is useless; or it depends
genuinely on the splitting, in which case---always, in every recorded
instance---one recovers the entire factorization from it by elementary
manipulation. What is never observed is an aggregate leaking a \emph{partial}
hint that would have to be amplified by further work.

The \emph{free-witness question} asks whether that observation is a theorem.
Call an aggregate a \emph{free witness} if an oracle handing you its value would
let you factor efficiently. The classification problem is: which aggregates are
free witnesses, which are factorization-insensitive, and is there anything in
between? This paper answers the question completely for the class of weights
that respect the Chinese Remainder splitting, and quantifies the cost of
computing such aggregates without an oracle.

\subsection{Summary of contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item The class of CRT weights and the factorization of the semiprime aggregate
through the CRT splitting, $A_w(pq)=(1+w(p))(1+w(q))$ (\S\ref{sec:crt}).
\item The trace lemma for strictly monotone CRT weights (\S\ref{sec:trace}).
\item Spread monotonicity of $a^{k}+b^{k}$ along the hyperbola $ab=N$, giving
uniqueness among \emph{all} factorizations, and the exhaustive dichotomy for the
family $\sigma_k$ (\S\ref{sec:power}).
\item A closed-form, constant-arithmetic-operation recovery formula, instantiated
at $k=1$ and at $k=2$---the latter being the predicted witness $p^{2}+q^{2}$
(\S\ref{sec:recovery}).
\item Prime collision as an absolute obstruction, and the two-branch dichotomy
for all CRT weights (\S\ref{sec:dichotomy}).
\item The characters-only boundary lemma (\S\ref{sec:boundary}).
\item The noise-floor principle and the identification of the aggregation
barrier with the birthday bound (\S\ref{sec:noise}).
\item The tropical reformulation (\S\ref{sec:tropical}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{CRT weights and their aggregates}\label{sec:crt}

Throughout, $N$ denotes a positive integer and $p,q$ denote primes. A
\emph{semiprime} is a product $N=pq$ of two distinct primes.

\begin{definition}[CRT weight]
A function $w:\N\to\N$ is a \emph{CRT weight} if
\begin{enumerate}
\item $w(1)=1$ (normalization), and
\item $w(mn)=w(m)\,w(n)$ for all $m,n$ with $\gcd(m,n)=1$ (multiplicativity
across the CRT splitting).
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

These are precisely the integer-valued multiplicative arithmetic functions. The
name emphasizes the mechanism: the ring isomorphism
$\Z/mn\cong\Z/m\times\Z/n$ for coprime $m,n$ is what makes counting functions
of this form arise naturally.

\begin{definition}[Aggregate]
The \emph{aggregate} of a CRT weight $w$ is $A_w(N)=\sum_{d\mid N}w(d)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Power weights]\label{ex:power}
For each $k\in\N$ the function $w_k(d)=d^{k}$ is a CRT weight: $w_k(1)=1$ and
$(mn)^{k}=m^{k}n^{k}$ for all $m,n$ (coprimality is not even needed). Its
aggregate is the divisor-power sum $\sigma_k(N)=\sum_{d\mid N}d^{k}$. For
$k\ge1$, $w_k$ is strictly monotone.
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}[The aggregate factors through the CRT splitting]
\label{prop:agg}
Let $w$ be a CRT weight and let $p\neq q$ be primes. Then
\[
A_w(pq)\;=\;\sum_{d\mid pq}w(d)\;=\;\bigl(1+w(p)\bigr)\bigl(1+w(q)\bigr).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The divisors of $pq$ for distinct primes $p,q$ are exactly $1,p,q,pq$, all
distinct (using $p,q\ge2$ and $p\neq q$, so $1<p<pq$ and $1<q<pq$). Hence
$A_w(pq)=w(1)+w(p)+w(q)+w(pq)$. Since $p\neq q$ are prime, $\gcd(p,q)=1$, so
$w(pq)=w(p)w(q)$; and $w(1)=1$. Therefore
$A_w(pq)=1+w(p)+w(q)+w(p)w(q)=(1+w(p))(1+w(q))$.
\end{proof}

Specializing to power weights gives $\sigma_k(pq)=(1+p^{k})(1+q^{k})$ and hence
the \emph{witness-extraction identity}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:extract}
p^{k}+q^{k}\;=\;\sigma_k(pq)-1-N^{k},\qquad N=pq,
\end{equation}
obtained by expanding $(1+p^{k})(1+q^{k})=1+(p^{k}+q^{k})+(pq)^{k}$. Both
subtracted quantities are computable from $N$ alone: the aggregate
\emph{hands over} the power-sum coordinate.

\section{The trace lemma in general form}\label{sec:trace}

\begin{lemma}[Sum and product determine an ordered pair]\label{lem:sumprod}
Let $x\le y$ and $x'\le y'$ be naturals with $x+y=x'+y'$ and $xy=x'y'$. Then
$x=x'$ and $y=y'$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $x<x'$ and set $t:=x'-x>0$. The sum condition gives $y'=y-t$, so
\[
xy=x'y'=(x+t)(y-t)=xy+t\,(y-x)-t^{2}=xy+t\,(y-x-t).
\]
Since $t>0$ this forces $y-x=t=x'-x$, i.e.\ $y=x'$, and then $y'=y-t=x'-t=x$.
But the hypotheses give $x'\le y'=x<x'$, a contradiction. By symmetry $x>x'$ is
impossible as well. Hence $x=x'$, and $y=y'$ follows from $x+y=x'+y'$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trace lemma for CRT weights]\label{thm:trace}
Let $w$ be a strictly monotone CRT weight. Let $N=ab=a'b'$ with
$\gcd(a,b)=\gcd(a',b')=1$, $a\le b$, $a'\le b'$. If
\[
\bigl(1+w(a)\bigr)\bigl(1+w(b)\bigr)=\bigl(1+w(a')\bigr)\bigl(1+w(b')\bigr),
\]
then $a=a'$ and $b=b'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Norms agree.} By multiplicativity on the coprime pairs,
$w(a)w(b)=w(N)=w(a')w(b')$.

\emph{Traces agree.} Expanding both sides of the hypothesis gives
$1+w(a)+w(b)+w(a)w(b)=1+w(a')+w(b')+w(a')w(b')$; cancelling the equal products
yields $w(a)+w(b)=w(a')+w(b')$.

\emph{Pair is pinned.} Monotonicity gives $w(a)\le w(b)$ and $w(a')\le w(b')$,
so Lemma~\ref{lem:sumprod} applied to $(w(a),w(b))$ and $(w(a'),w(b'))$ yields
$w(a)=w(a')$. Strict monotonicity implies injectivity, so $a=a'$. Finally
$ab=a'b'=ab'$; if $a>0$ we cancel to get $b=b'$, while if $a=0$ then coprimality
forces $b=b'=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The quantity $w(a)w(b)=w(N)$ is the \emph{norm}: public, computable from $N$
alone, identical for all factorizations. The quantity $w(a)+w(b)$ is the
\emph{trace}: exactly what the aggregate reveals beyond the norm.
Theorem~\ref{thm:trace} says the trace is a \emph{factor-secret coordinate}---a
single number equivalent to the factorization. This is the precise sense in
which recoverable counting witnesses collapse to one coordinate.
\end{remark}

\begin{corollary}
For $k\ge1$ and coprime factorizations $N=ab=a'b'$ with $a\le b$, $a'\le b'$,
the equality $(1+a^{k})(1+b^{k})=(1+a'^{k})(1+b'^{k})$ forces $a=a'$, $b=b'$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The sharp form for power weights, and the \texorpdfstring{$\sigma_k$}{sigma-k} dichotomy}
\label{sec:power}

\begin{lemma}[Spread monotonicity, linear case]\label{lem:spread1}
If $ab=a'b'$ with $a<a'$ and $a'\le b'$, then $a'+b'<a+b$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Since $ab=a'b'$ and $a<a'$ we have $b>b'$. Write $a'=a+s$ with $s>0$ and
$b=b'+t$ with $t>0$. Then $ab=a'b'$ reads $a(b'+t)=(a+s)b'$, i.e.\ $at=sb'$.
Since $a<a'\le b'$ we get $at=sb'\ge sa'>sa$, hence $t>s$, hence
$a+b=a+b'+t>a+s+b'=a'+b'$.
\end{proof}

Geometrically: among rectangles of fixed area, the more lopsided one has the
larger semiperimeter.

\begin{theorem}[Spread monotonicity for power sums]\label{thm:spreadk}
Let $k\ge1$ and $0<a<a'\le b'<b$ with $ab=a'b'$. Then
$a'^{k}+b'^{k}<a^{k}+b^{k}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:spread1}, $a'+b'<a+b$, i.e.\ $t:=a'-a<u:=b-b'$, with
$t,u>0$. Using the telescoping identity
$x^{k}-y^{k}=\bigl(\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}x^{i}y^{\,k-1-i}\bigr)(x-y)$ we write
$a'^{k}-a^{k}=C_1t$ and $b^{k}-b'^{k}=C_2u$ with
$C_1=\sum_{i<k}a'^{i}a^{\,k-1-i}$ and $C_2=\sum_{i<k}b^{i}b'^{\,k-1-i}$.
Since $a'\le b'<b$ and $a<a'\le b'$, every term of $C_1$ is at most the
corresponding term of $C_2$, so $0<C_1\le C_2$; positivity uses $a>0$ and
$k\ge1$, so the sum is nonempty. Therefore
$a'^{k}-a^{k}=C_1t<C_2u=b^{k}-b'^{k}$, which rearranges to the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Trace lemma for power sums, all factorizations]\label{thm:tracek}
Let $k\ge1$ and $N=ab=a'b'$ with $0<a\le b$ and $0<a'\le b'$. If
$a^{k}+b^{k}=a'^{k}+b'^{k}$ then $a=a'$ and $b=b'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $a<a'$ then $b>b'$ (the products agree), so $a<a'\le b'<b$ and
Theorem~\ref{thm:spreadk} gives a strict inequality, contradicting equality.
Symmetrically $a>a'$ is impossible. Hence $a=a'$, and $b=b'$ by cancellation.
\end{proof}

This is strictly stronger than the coprime case: the power-sum witness
identifies the factorization among \emph{all} divisor pairs.

\begin{proposition}[The $k=0$ aggregate is factorization-insensitive]
\label{prop:sigma0}
For all distinct primes $p,q$ one has $\sigma_0(pq)=4$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\sigma_0$ counts divisors, and a semiprime with distinct prime factors has
exactly the four divisors $1,p,q,pq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exhaustive dichotomy for the power family]\label{thm:sigmadich}
For each exponent $k\in\N$ exactly one of the following holds.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textup{(Insensitive, $k=0$.)} $\sigma_k$ takes the same value on every
semiprime with distinct prime factors.
\item \textup{(Free witness, $k\ge1$.)} For every $N$ and every pair of
factorizations $N=ab=a'b'$ with $0<a\le b$, $0<a'\le b'$, the equality
$a^{k}+b^{k}=a'^{k}+b'^{k}$ implies $(a,b)=(a',b')$.
\end{enumerate}
There is no intermediate, partially informative member of the family.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $k=0$ apply Proposition~\ref{prop:sigma0}; if $k\ge1$ apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:tracek}.
\end{proof}

\section{Explicit recovery: the trace coordinate is efficiently invertible}
\label{sec:recovery}

\begin{definition}
For naturals $N$ (the norm) and $s$ (the trace) set
\[
R(N,s)\;=\;\Bigl\lfloor \tfrac12\bigl(s-\lfloor\sqrt{\,s^{2}-4N\,}\rfloor\bigr)\Bigr\rfloor,
\]
all operations in $\N$ (truncated subtraction, integer square root, integer
halving).
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Closed-form recovery from trace and norm]\label{thm:recover}
For all naturals $a\le b$ one has $R(ab,\;a+b)=a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $b=a+c$ with $c\ge0$. Then $(a+b)^{2}-4ab=(2a+c)^{2}-4a(a+c)=c^{2}$, so
the integer square root is exactly $c$, with no truncation loss. Hence
$R(ab,a+b)=\lfloor\tfrac12((2a+c)-c)\rfloor=a$.
\end{proof}

This is the integer form of the quadratic formula
$x=\tfrac12\bigl(s-\sqrt{s^{2}-4N}\bigr)$, and it uses $O(1)$ arithmetic
operations on integers of size $O(N)$.

\begin{corollary}[Recovery from the divisor sum]\label{cor:sig1}
Let $p<q$ be primes and $N=pq$. Then $R\bigl(N,\ \sigma_1(N)-1-N\bigr)=p$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:extract} with $k=1$, $\sigma_1(N)-1-N=p+q$; apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:recover} with $a=p$, $b=q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Recovery from the sum of squares---the predicted witness]
\label{cor:sig2}
Let $p<q$ be primes and $N=pq$. Then
\[
R\Bigl(N,\ \bigl\lfloor\sqrt{\,\sigma_2(N)-1-N^{2}+2N\,}\bigr\rfloor\Bigr)=p.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
By \eqref{eq:extract} with $k=2$, $\sigma_2(N)-1-N^{2}=p^{2}+q^{2}$. Adding
$2N=2pq$ gives $(p+q)^{2}$ exactly, so the integer square root returns $p+q$
with no truncation. Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:recover}.
\end{proof}

Corollary~\ref{cor:sig2} is the formal content of the prediction that
$\sigma_k(N)=(1+p^{k})(1+q^{k})$ is a free witness for every $k\ge1$: the case
$k=2$ was singled out in advance because $p^{2}+q^{2}$ superficially looks
\emph{weaker} than $p+q$, and the theory said it could not be. It is not---the
norm supplies the missing $2pq$.

\begin{proposition}[Recovery from the max coordinate]
For $0<p\le q$ one has $\lfloor pq/\max(p,q)\rfloor=\min(p,q)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\max(p,q)=q$ and $pq/q=p=\min(p,q)$ exactly.
\end{proof}

Thus the recoverable coordinates observed in practice---$p+q$, $\max(p,q)$, and
residue or order vectors---are all one division or one quadratic formula away
from the factorization.

\section{The negative branch and the exhaustive dichotomy}\label{sec:dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Prime collision is an absolute obstruction]\label{thm:collision}
Let $w$ be a CRT weight, and suppose there exist distinct primes $p\neq p'$ with
$w(p)=w(p')$. Then there is \emph{no} function $f:\N\to\N$ satisfying
$f\bigl(A_w(xy)\bigr)=x$ for all primes $x<y$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since there are infinitely many primes, choose a prime $q>\max(p,p')$. Both
$pq$ and $p'q$ are semiprimes with distinct prime factors, and by
Proposition~\ref{prop:agg},
\[
A_w(pq)=(1+w(p))(1+w(q))=(1+w(p'))(1+w(q))=A_w(p'q).
\]
If such an $f$ existed, applying it to this common value would give both $p$ and
$p'$, forcing $p=p'$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Note the strength: $f$ is an \emph{arbitrary} function, with no computability or
complexity restriction. The obstruction is informational, not computational.

\begin{theorem}[Exhaustive dichotomy for CRT weights]\label{thm:dich}
Let $w$ be a CRT weight. Then at least one of the following holds.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textup{(Separating.)} $w$ is injective on primes.
\item \textup{(Blind.)} There is no function $f$ with $f(A_w(xy))=x$ for all
primes $x<y$.
\end{enumerate}
Moreover, if $w$ is strictly monotone then case~(1) holds and, by
Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}, the aggregate pins the factorization; and if $w$ is not
injective on primes then case~(2) holds and no recovery is possible at all.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Either $w$ is injective on primes, giving (1), or it is not, in which case
Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} gives (2). A strictly monotone $w$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strictly monotone CRT weights are always free witnesses]
Let $w$ be a strictly monotone CRT weight and let $p<q$, $p'<q'$ be primes with
$pq=p'q'$ and $A_w(pq)=A_w(p'q')$. Then $p=p'$ and $q=q'$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Rewrite both aggregates by Proposition~\ref{prop:agg} and apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:trace} with $(a,b)=(p,q)$, $(a',b')=(p',q')$, the coprimality
coming from distinctness of primes.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Reading of the classification]
Within the CRT class there is no middle ground. A weight either separates
primes, in which case (under the mild additional hypothesis of monotonicity)
its aggregate is worth the entire factorization; or it collides, in which case
its aggregate is worth nothing. Every counting aggregate in this class is
therefore either a \emph{complete} witness or a \emph{null} witness. This
dissolves what looked like nine distinct experimental phenomena into one
mechanism: a counting aggregate over a CRT-separable domain, with
non-polynomial local weights, jointly encoding both factors and hence dodging
the symmetry obstruction, sealed only by the cost of computing it.
\end{remark}

\section{The characters-only boundary}\label{sec:boundary}

\begin{proposition}
For every $k$ and all $m,n$ one has $(mn)^{k}=m^{k}n^{k}$; power weights lie in
the class.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Characters-only boundary lemma]\label{thm:boundary}
Let $z$ be a nonzero complex number and suppose the exponential phase weight
$x\mapsto z^{x}$ is CRT-multiplicative, i.e.\ $z^{mn}=z^{m}z^{n}$ for all
coprime $m,n$. Then $z=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the hypothesis to the coprime pair $(2,3)$: $z^{6}=z^{2}z^{3}=z^{5}$.
Since $z\neq0$ we have $z^{5}\neq0$, and dividing gives $z=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Integer shadow]
For an integer $c\ge2$ the weight $x\mapsto c^{x}$ is not CRT-multiplicative:
the pair $(2,3)$ would force $c^{6}=c^{5}$, impossible since $c^{5}<c^{6}$.
\end{corollary}

Exponential phases---the weights driving Fourier analysis, Gauss sums, and the
analytic toolkit---are structurally \emph{additive} in the exponent, whereas the
CRT splitting is \emph{multiplicative} in the argument. The two are incompatible
except trivially. Oscillatory attacks based on phase functions therefore do not
decompose through the Chinese Remainder Theorem at all, and the natural class of
aggregates that do is exactly the class of multiplicative characters---the class
the dichotomy of \S\ref{sec:dichotomy} covers.

\section{The noise-floor principle}\label{sec:noise}

Sections \ref{sec:trace}--\ref{sec:dichotomy} concern the \emph{information} in
an aggregate. This section concerns the \emph{cost} of obtaining it, and it is
here that the positive branch is sealed. Evaluating $A_w(N)=\sum_{d\mid N}w(d)$
requires knowing the divisors of $N$. Absent an oracle one sweeps a window of
probes $d=2,3,\dots,m$ and tests divisibility.

\subsection{The exact count below the corner}

\begin{theorem}[Noise floor below the tropical corner]\label{thm:window}
Let $p<q$ be primes and $N=pq$. Then
\[
\{\,d\in[2,\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor]\ :\ d\mid N\,\}=\{p\},
\qquad
\bigl|[2,\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor]\bigr|=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor-1,
\]
so the density of factor-bearing probes below the corner is exactly
$1/(\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor-1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $p<q$ we have $p^{2}<pq=N$, so $p\le\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$; and $p\ge2$,
so $p$ lies in the window and divides $N$. Conversely let $d$ lie in the window
with $d\mid N$. The divisors of $N$ are $1,p,q,N$. We exclude $d=1$ (below the
window) and $d\in\{q,N\}$, because $d\le\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$ implies
$d^{2}\le N$ whereas $q^{2}>pq=N$ and $N^{2}>N$. So $d=p$. The cardinality of
$[2,M]$ is $M-1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The balanced case with explicit constants}

\begin{definition}
A semiprime $N=pq$ with $p<q$ is \emph{balanced} if $q\le2p$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bal}
If $N=pq$ is balanced then $N\le2p^{2}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$pq\le p(2p)=2p^{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Every nontrivial divisor is at least $p$]\label{lem:ge}
If $N=pq$ with $p<q$ primes and $d\mid N$ with $1<d<N$, then $d\in\{p,q\}$; in
particular $d\ge p$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The divisor list is $1,p,q,N$, and the two extremes are excluded.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The sweep must reach the birthday scale]\label{thm:sweep}
Let $N=pq$ be a balanced semiprime and let $[2,m]$ be a probe window containing
some $d$ with $d\mid N$ and $1<d<N$. Then $N\le2m^{2}$, equivalently
$\sqrt N\le\sqrt2\,m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:ge}, $p\le d\le m$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:bal},
$N\le2p^{2}\le2m^{2}$. Taking square roots gives $\sqrt N\le\sqrt2\,m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded numerator]\label{thm:num}
For any $m$ and any semiprime $N=pq$ with $p<q$ primes,
$\bigl|\{\,d\in[2,m]:d\mid N,\ d\neq N\,\}\bigr|\le2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Any such $d$ is a divisor of $N$ other than $1$ and $N$, hence lies in
$\{p,q\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Noise-floor principle]\label{thm:noise}
Let $N=pq$ be a balanced semiprime and let $[2,m]$ be a probe window containing
a nontrivial divisor of $N$. Then
\[
\frac{\bigl|\{\,d\in[2,m]:d\mid N,\ d\neq N\,\}\bigr|}{m}
\;\le\;\frac{2\sqrt2}{\sqrt N}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:num} the numerator is at most $2$, so the left side is at
most $2/m$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sweep}, $m\ge\sqrt N/\sqrt2$, whence
$2/m\le2\sqrt2/\sqrt N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Aggregation cost is exactly the birthday scale]\label{thm:cost}
For a balanced semiprime $N=pq$ with $p<q$, the sweep length $p$ satisfies
$\sqrt{N/2}\le p\le\sqrt N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The upper bound is $p^{2}<pq=N$; the lower bound is Lemma~\ref{lem:bal}.
\end{proof}

Thus the aggregation barrier---the cost of evaluating a counting aggregate
without an oracle---and the trial-division birthday bound are the \emph{same}
$\Theta(\sqrt N)$ obstruction, with noise density pinned at $c/\sqrt N$ with the
explicit constant $c=2\sqrt2$. A free witness is free only if someone gives it
to you.

\subsection{No fixed probe set suffices}

\begin{theorem}[Finite probe sets fail]\label{thm:probes}
For every finite $S\subset\N$ there exist primes $p<q$ such that no $s\in S$ is
a nontrivial divisor of $N=pq$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $M=\max S$ (or $M=0$ if $S=\emptyset$). Choose a prime $p>M$ and a prime
$q>p$. The nontrivial divisors of $N=pq$ are $p$ and $q$, both exceeding
$M\ge s$ for every $s\in S$.
\end{proof}

\section{The tropical reformulation}\label{sec:tropical}

The min-plus, or \emph{tropical}, semiring replaces $x\oplus y:=\min(x,y)$ and
$x\odot y:=x+y$. Under this coordinate change, the multiplicative constraint
$ab=N$ becomes a tropical relation $X\odot Y=\widetilde N$: a \emph{tropical
line}, a piecewise-linear curve of two rays meeting at a corner. The corner is
the balanced point $a=b=\sqrt N$. Every factorization of $N$ is a point on this
line.

\begin{theorem}[The balanced pair minimizes the trace]\label{thm:corner}
Let $N>0$ and $N=ab=a'b'$ with $a\le b$, $a'\le b'$ and $a\le a'$. Then
$a'+b'\le a+b$; equivalently, in the tropical semiring,
$\trop(a+b)\oplus\trop(a'+b')=\trop(a'+b')$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $a=a'$ then cancelling in $ab=a'b'$ gives $b=b'$ (using $a>0$, which follows
from $ab=N>0$), so the traces are equal. If $a<a'$, Lemma~\ref{lem:spread1}
gives the strict inequality.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The corner sees the factors]
For primes $p<q$ and $N=pq$ one has $p+q<1+N$, and hence
$\trop(1+N)\oplus\trop(p+q)=\trop(p+q)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$1+pq-p-q=(p-1)(q-1)>0$ since $p,q\ge2$.
\end{proof}

The tropical picture unifies the two halves of the paper. \emph{Trace lemma,
tropically:} the factoring secret is nothing more than a position on the
tropical line $X\odot Y=\widetilde N$; Theorem~\ref{thm:trace} says that any
strictly monotone CRT weight simply re-coordinatizes that line, so different
counting attacks are different rulers laid along the same line.
\emph{Aggregation barrier, tropically:} Theorem~\ref{thm:cost} says the position
of the prime factorization lies within a factor $\sqrt2$ of the corner, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:window} says exactly one probe in the entire window below the
corner is useful. Locating the corner costs a sweep of length
$\Theta(\sqrt N)$.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Oracle recovery / free-witness inversion]
\emph{Input:} $N$, an exponent $k\ge1$, and the aggregate $A=\sigma_k(N)$ for a
semiprime $N=pq$. \emph{Output:} $p$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $T_k\leftarrow A-1-N^{k}$; by \eqref{eq:extract} this equals $p^{k}+q^{k}$.
\item If $k=1$ set $s\leftarrow T_1$; if $k=2$ set
$s\leftarrow\lfloor\sqrt{T_2+2N}\rfloor$, which equals $p+q$ exactly.
\item Return $R(N,s)$.
\end{enumerate}
Correctness is Corollaries~\ref{cor:sig1}--\ref{cor:sig2}; cost is $O(1)$
arithmetic operations, i.e.\ polynomial in $\log N$. For general $k\ge3$ the
trace is recovered from $T_k$ and $N$ by a monotone binary search on $s$, using
Theorem~\ref{thm:spreadk} for monotonicity, at cost $O(\log N)$ evaluations.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Aggregate evaluation by sweeping]
\emph{Input:} $N$ and a weight $w$. \emph{Output:} $A_w(N)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $A\leftarrow w(1)+w(N)$.
\item For $d=2,\dots,\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$: if $d\mid N$ then
$A\leftarrow A+w(d)+w(N/d)$, with a correction when $d^{2}=N$.
\item Return $A$.
\end{enumerate}
Cost: $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ divisibility tests in the worst case, exponential in
$\log N$. Theorems~\ref{thm:sweep} and \ref{thm:cost} show this is unavoidable
for balanced semiprimes, and Theorem~\ref{thm:probes} shows no fixed finite
probe set replaces it.
\end{algorithmbox}

The pair of algorithms is the whole story: the first is free and fast, the
second is necessary and slow, and their composition is exactly trial division.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What is established}

Within the CRT-multiplicative class the classification is complete: every
aggregate on a semiprime factors as $(1+w(p))(1+w(q))$, so both factors are
jointly encoded (Proposition~\ref{prop:agg}); monotone weights give aggregates
that determine the factorization (Theorem~\ref{thm:trace}) with explicit $O(1)$
recovery (Theorem~\ref{thm:recover}), sharply so for power weights
(Theorem~\ref{thm:tracek}); colliding weights give aggregates from which no
function whatsoever recovers a factor (Theorem~\ref{thm:collision}); these are
the only two behaviours (Theorem~\ref{thm:dich}); the class is exactly the
multiplicative characters (Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary}); and the positive branch
is sealed by a $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ aggregation cost with explicit noise density
$\le2\sqrt2/\sqrt N$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:sweep}--\ref{thm:cost}).

\subsection{What is not established}

Nothing here proves that integer factorization is computationally hard; that
remains open, and no argument of this type could settle it, since the results
concern a specific---if broad---class of approaches. In particular: the
classification covers weights multiplicative on coprime arguments, so aggregates
whose local data are vectors rather than scalars, or that are multiplicative
only up to a controlled error, are not covered; the noise-floor principle is
proved here for divisor-probe aggregates on balanced semiprimes, its general
form remaining an empirical regularity observed across several independent
settings at the $c/\sqrt N$ scale; and Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} rules out
recovery from the aggregate \emph{value alone}, so attacks exploiting the
internal geometry of a counting problem rather than its cardinality are outside
the model.

\subsection{Relation to the broader barrier framework}

The results make precise three informal barriers. \emph{Factorization-insensitivity}
is Theorem~\ref{thm:collision} together with Proposition~\ref{prop:sigma0}. The
\emph{symmetry barrier}---that a witness must break the symmetry between the two
factors---is dodged precisely by the CRT factorization of
Proposition~\ref{prop:agg}. And the \emph{aggregation barrier} is
Theorems~\ref{thm:sweep}--\ref{thm:cost}, now identified with the birthday bound
rather than merely compared to it.

\section{Future directions}

The declared frontier is to prove trace-lemma exhaustiveness in full generality:
define the class of CRT-respecting, polynomial-time-computable counting
functions---already sharply delimited by the characters-only boundary
lemma---and prove the dichotomy that any member is either
factorization-insensitive or reduces to a factor-secret coordinate with
polynomial-time recovery. The present paper does this for CRT-multiplicative
weights; the general statement would upgrade the aggregation barrier to a
statement equivalent to factoring hardness. Concrete next steps:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Vector-valued and higher-rank weights.} Extend the dichotomy to
weights valued in a commutative monoid or a lattice, where ``collides on two
primes'' becomes a statement about the kernel of the induced map.
\item \textbf{Approximate multiplicativity.} Prove a stability version: if
$w(mn)=w(m)w(n)(1+O(\varepsilon))$ on coprime arguments, does the dichotomy
survive with a quantitative loss?
\item \textbf{The noise-floor principle as a theorem.} Establish the
$c/\sqrt N$ density bound for a general class of $N$-computable aggregates and
identify the optimal constant.
\item \textbf{Barrier equivalence.} Formulate and attempt the statement that the
aggregation barrier is equivalent to factoring hardness within the CRT class.
\item \textbf{Unbalanced semiprimes.} A uniform quantitative statement covering
all semiprimes, not only those with $q\le2p$.
\item \textbf{Beyond semiprimes.} For general $N=\prod p_i^{e_i}$ the aggregate
factors as $\prod_i(1+w(p_i)+\dots+w(p_i^{e_i}))$; the trace/norm argument
becomes a symmetric-function argument. Does the aggregate still determine the
factorization with more than two prime factors, and at what cost?
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

For counting attacks whose local weights decompose through the Chinese Remainder
splitting the landscape is now fully mapped. There are exactly two kinds: those
whose aggregate is worth the entire factorization, and those whose aggregate is
worth nothing. The first is characterized by prime separation, comes with a
closed-form inversion built from the trace and the norm, and includes the
predicted witness $p^{2}+q^{2}$. The second is blocked absolutely, by an
information-theoretic collision rather than a complexity assumption. No third
behaviour exists, and the boundary of the class is exactly the multiplicative
characters. The free witnesses are sealed by cost, not information: evaluating
one requires a sweep of length $\Theta(\sqrt N)$ with factor-bearing probes at
density at most $2\sqrt2/\sqrt N$. In tropical coordinates the picture reduces to
a sentence: the factoring secret is a position on the tropical line
$X\odot Y=N$, every counting witness in the class is a ruler along that line,
the position lies within $\sqrt2$ of the corner, and walking to the corner costs
exactly what it has always cost.

